WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Hi everyone, it's Jerry.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.000
I have an interesting game to share with you that was played on Chess.com December 6th,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.880
a 3-0 Blitz game between Magnus Carlsen on the white end and Daniel Naroditsky.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.600
This was Carlsen's last opponent on his path to breaking Nakamura's Blitz record.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.760
They played a group of six games.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.280
This is the one I found most interesting and instructive.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:35.320
The Blitz record was 3336, Carlsen is now at the top with a 3340 Blitz rating.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.560
So it's really cool to see the competition between these two.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.800
Each, you can be sure, is well aware of these records and trying to one-up each other.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.000
So in the coming weeks, months, will Nakamura one-up Carlsen?

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.800
We'll have to see.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.200
Some World Chess Championship prep for this game?

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.880
A serious opening to be sure, Ruy Lopez.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.680
Now one of the things I found instructive with this game is this piece right here.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:15.840
Watch how this one minor piece, when posted on rank 6, dominates not just one, but two

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.360
major pieces.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.640
Let's get some standard moves in here.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:28.280
Once white secures e4, there is a threat to win a pawn.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.560
Black's there in time, kicks the bishop back.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:37.320
d6, prep work for the central push, new square for the bishop.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:43.200
Castles, security for the knight prior to d4.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.040
Chigorin variation, bishop is preserved, c-pawn contributes.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.560
This is one of the points behind the Chigorin, get the c-pawn playing.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:59.440
In comes d4, knight d7 signals the Keres variation.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:07.960
Space now for Carlsen on the queenside, bishop f6, knight b to d2, and in the game we now

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.560
have g6.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.840
This is one of the most popular moves in this position.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.280
In the computer's eyes, what's considered best is knight b6.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.320
For what reason or reasons?

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:29.920
Well, a knight on b6 factors in two pawn advances, one of them being b4.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.560
Black's now in a position to consider knight c4, has support for that jump.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:43.720
The other note is that this knight on b6 has an eye on a4, so this thematic pawn advance

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:51.880
a4, rook pawn attacking a knight pawn to activate a rook, it's not as effective, or

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:59.480
I should say if you do play a4 here, there's now a cost, and that cost would be the light

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.120
square bishop.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:10.080
Alright, in this game it's g6, right around the corner for black, bishop g7 and f5?

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.040
We continue now with b4, and in the game the knight goes back to b7.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.860
If the knight tries to go forward here, this is not good because white can take.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:27.200
This pawn is especially vulnerable, probably an overextended pawn, likely to fall.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:34.480
But on top of that, a big square is given up to white with this capture, a4.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:41.960
The bishop is now able to snake into the weakest square in black's camp, c6.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:52.040
In this game it's knight b7, in comes a4, cb, cb, and now b takes a is played.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:04:00.680
So you may be wondering, why is black welcoming this bishop into the weakest square, c6?

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.520
What if black doesn't take on a4 here?

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.280
Let's say the rook goes to b8, preparing to meet this capture with the recapture.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.080
The rook is no longer in trouble.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:20.320
If this ram structure is present, how does this fianchettoed knight contribute?

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.060
How do you undermine the b-pawn?

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.140
This is black's idea in the game.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.320
He's well aware that this bishop is being activated.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.920
His idea is to now challenge the b-pawn so that these two knights finally have access

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.400
to c5.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.760
In comes bishop c6.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.640
Two things happening with that move.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.080
There's two pins, one move, two pins here.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.200
Pawn and knight are now pinned to the rook.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:05:00.960
From here rook b8, and now the bishop with b5 is officially superglued into this c6 square.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.040
This is already a decisive advantage for white.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.000
Very accurate game by Carlsen here.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:18.240
The problem here, one of the problems for black, is the bishop is annoying, and if you

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.640
try to get rid of the bishop, you're going to have to deal with an annoying pawn.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.360
Maybe taking it towards the center, or away from the center, acquiring connected passed

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.760
pawns.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.920
So however you slice it, a fantastic position for white here.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:40.040
A bishop or pawn on c6 is a great thorn in black's side.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:46.840
I said earlier that the bishop dominates two major pieces.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.400
We're going to see that much more clearly as the position simplifies, but you may say

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.720
the bishop on c6 dominates the whole board.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.280
It's that good of a piece.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:08.520
From here we continue with knight b6, bishop a3 has an eye on a base point, a4, queen c2,

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.040
bishop e7, and knight c4.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.400
Black is only playing on three ranks really.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:20.320
This is the furthest advanced pawn, and even that point is being challenged.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:27.100
We get that knight exchange, and some context here with this three minute game.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.880
Black spent about 25 seconds on this next move.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:36.980
He played queen b6, and Carlsen's reply came in about a second.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:41.760
He had already anticipated that this would be a move, and that the bishop on e7 would

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.720
be unprotected.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.800
In comes knight e7.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.600
If you take, you lose the bishop.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.960
Part of Naroditsky's calculation, I imagine, was to meet this move.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.760
He saw it was coming, and his idea was this counterpunch.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.160
Carlsen saw that one because his reply was nearly instant.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.760
He drops right back to f3.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:07.240
He's prepared in this position to part ways with a rook.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:08.240
Why?

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.720
Well, this is a super valuable dark square bishop.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:18.680
You might want to think about that fianchettoed bishop as a four-point bishop.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.840
Now there are these ideas to do something on the dark squares.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.080
Line the queen and bishop up, go for mate.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.120
Completely winning position here for white.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.360
There is a follow up here with knight c5.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:36.840
We can simplify, and we have now no queens on board.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:37.840
Look at this piece.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:43.440
This is where it becomes crystal clear of just how amazing this bishop is.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:49.160
We have these files for the rooks, but how do you get there with this monster on c6?

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:54.000
Completely dominant position, and with this last pawn capture, there is now a second passed

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.720
pawn for white.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.480
The d-pawn is ready to go.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:07.580
From here, rook takes a, bishop b7, knight e5, possible fork here.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.320
Rook a8.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:15.300
Normally, you'll want to duck the rook exchange when you're behind the exchange here.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:22.180
Keep a rook on board, help out with the minor pieces, but white is in such a great position

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:25.740
here, you could actually simplify this position further.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.020
He exchanges rooks, and gets the king rolling.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:35.260
There's a clear plan to bring the king all the way up here, take a pawn, and start pushing.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.540
There's nothing that black can do.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:45.140
He tries f5, knight d7, targets the rook, rook d8, and now a cool move here, knight

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:46.140
b6.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.140
He's saying, go ahead, take me.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:54.060
I'm going to be able to recapture with either pawn, and then there's no stopping my c-pawn.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:09:00.500
So Naroditsky comes up with something clever here, bishop b7, his plan to meet the capture

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:01.740
with a skewer.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:09.740
He gets the piece back, but white preserves that monster light square bishop.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:20.260
It's once more glued to the c6 square, and from here we now have a third passed pawn.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.260
This one's over.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:23.780
What do you do here?

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:28.660
We get a couple more moves in, but three passed pawns too many.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.120
The plan here, scoop up the pawn.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:37.000
We just get a couple king moves in, but there's just no activity for the rook, and white is

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.240
completely winning.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:42.100
This is the point that black threw in the towel.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:48.120
If the game did continue, we're taking here and just pushing all of our pawns.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.800
There's really not a way to go wrong here.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:57.520
Zero activity for black, zero counterplay for black in this final position.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:59.240
What did you think of this one?

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:04.560
Anyhow, feel free, as usual, to leave any feedback to this video in the comments section

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:05.560
below.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:08.680
I hope you enjoyed it, and maybe took a thing or two away.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:09.680
That's all for now.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:10.680
Take care.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:11.180
Bye.

